WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.049
This is the executive briefing
for Network and Transport,

3
00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:07.200
one of the books of the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:12.105
It's for the people who fund, authorize
and answer for the branch network:

5
00:00:12.105 --> 00:00:16.512
the CIO, the CISO, network program
executives and budget owners.

6
00:00:16.512 --> 00:00:19.587
It's built around decisions,
not configuration.

7
00:00:19.587 --> 00:00:24.612
For each one, I'll say what has to be
decided, who owns it, and when it's needed.

8
00:00:24.612 --> 00:00:26.988
Four questions organize the half hour.

9
00:00:26.988 --> 00:00:30.463
Why act now, when the target
architecture is years away.

10
00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:34.800
What federal policy actually requires,
and where the money should go first.

11
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.463
Who decides each step.

12
00:00:36.463 --> 00:00:40.475
And how to proceed without turning
a faster network into a weaker one.

13
00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:41.812
Here's the short version.

14
00:00:41.812 --> 00:00:44.149
No product shortens a fiber path.

15
00:00:44.149 --> 00:00:49.300
So buy transport on physics, govern it as
compliance, and decide in the right order.

16
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.690
Let's start with the decisions
this briefing asks for.

17
00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.502
This briefing asks for seven
decisions, each with a named owner.

18
00:01:00.502 --> 00:01:04.015
None is a product choice, and
none needs an award to start.

19
00:01:04.015 --> 00:01:08.065
Several cost nothing but a signature
and a few weeks of staff time.

20
00:01:08.065 --> 00:01:12.173
The expensive mistakes come from
leaving them unmade while circuits,

21
00:01:12.173 --> 00:01:15.715
refreshes and migrations move
ahead on their own schedules.

22
00:01:15.715 --> 00:01:18.712
In short: the CISO ends
managed TIC circuits

23
00:01:18.712 --> 00:01:22.277
only when CISA confirms
the replacement telemetry.

24
00:01:22.277 --> 00:01:26.388
The authorizing official records
today's gap as an accepted risk,

25
00:01:26.388 --> 00:01:28.139
before an assessor finds it.

26
00:01:28.139 --> 00:01:32.068
Security architecture
writes the target into the

27
00:01:32.068 --> 00:01:36.090
edge-device refresh that
BOD 26-02 is forcing anyway.

28
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:40.625
The sponsor and CIO put staff time
and requirements work before capital,

29
00:01:40.625 --> 00:01:45.764
test the field-office refresh request
against published capacity limits, and tie

30
00:01:45.764 --> 00:01:50.752
the retirement of the Windows RADIUS server
to the Entra migration, not to a date.

31
00:01:50.752 --> 00:01:55.205
And the authorizing official and
system owner hold the line on order:

32
00:01:55.205 --> 00:01:57.940
the governor is live before traffic moves.

33
00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.177
The thread through all seven is simple.

34
00:02:00.177 --> 00:02:03.458
Policy names no product, but
it isn't requirement-free,

35
00:02:03.458 --> 00:02:06.141
and order and ownership
decide the outcome.

36
00:02:06.141 --> 00:02:08.040
First, why act at all.

37
00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:16.365
Programs keep merging two different
decisions, and that's where the risk starts.

38
00:02:16.365 --> 00:02:18.765
Choosing a pipe is a physics decision.

39
00:02:18.765 --> 00:02:23.115
Light in fiber covers about a
kilometer every five microseconds.

40
00:02:23.115 --> 00:02:27.249
The voice budget leaves the network
roughly 70 to 100 milliseconds,

41
00:02:27.249 --> 00:02:31.752
and on the book's worked arithmetic,
a Seattle office hairpinning through

42
00:02:31.752 --> 00:02:36.402
northern Virginia spends about 60
milliseconds one way on geography alone.

43
00:02:36.402 --> 00:02:38.240
No product moves that floor.

44
00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.077
Local breakout fixes the
geographic part, and nothing else.

45
00:02:42.077 --> 00:02:45.327
Choosing what governs the pipe
is a compliance decision.

46
00:02:45.327 --> 00:02:48.080
Direct internet access solves the hairpin,

47
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.215
but it satisfies none of
the protection controls.

48
00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:56.077
Boundary protection, inspection and
monitoring all come from the control layer.

49
00:02:56.077 --> 00:02:58.502
So here's what an
executive should watch for.

50
00:02:58.502 --> 00:03:01.183
A branch moved from a
governed hairpin to bare

51
00:03:01.183 --> 00:03:04.428
local breakout has lost
assessed boundary protection.

52
00:03:04.428 --> 00:03:08.103
The book calls that a compliance
regression with better latency.

53
00:03:08.103 --> 00:03:10.860
It looks like modernization
on a dashboard,

54
00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.464
and it reads as a
finding in an assessment.

55
00:03:13.464 --> 00:03:17.409
Every decision here keeps those two
choices separate: buy the pipe on

56
00:03:17.409 --> 00:03:21.353
measured physics, and take the
protection from the governor above it.

57
00:03:21.353 --> 00:03:23.940
Next, the clocks already running.

58
00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:31.803
The second reason to act is that the
external clocks are already running,

59
00:03:31.803 --> 00:03:34.203
whether or not a network
program is funded.

60
00:03:34.203 --> 00:03:40.355
FIPS 140-2 certificates moved to NIST's
historical list on September 22,

61
00:03:40.355 --> 00:03:44.839
so new purchases should
state the 140-3 expectation.

62
00:03:44.839 --> 00:03:53.477
The post-quantum memo, M-26-15, wanted
agency migration plans by October 22, 2026.

63
00:03:53.477 --> 00:03:59.639
The first agency logging plan
under M-26-14 is due November 18.

64
00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:06.467
BOD 26-04's third phase lands December
7, FedRAMP's 2026 rules become

65
00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:13.790
mandatory on January 1, and the EIS
first option period ends July 31, 2027.

66
00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:18.053
The one that spends money is BOD 26-02.

67
00:04:18.053 --> 00:04:21.684
It requires identified end-of-support edge

68
00:04:21.684 --> 00:04:25.415
devices off agency networks
by August 5, 2027.

69
00:04:25.415 --> 00:04:27.558
I'll come back to it,
because it's the fork

70
00:04:27.558 --> 00:04:30.103
in the road for the whole
branch architecture.

71
00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:31.439
Here's the honest part.

72
00:04:31.439 --> 00:04:35.742
Three of these requirements fall inside
roughly fifteen months, and they all

73
00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:39.977
draw on the same network and security
staff a branch-edge program would need.

74
00:04:39.977 --> 00:04:43.041
An agency promising an
SD-WAN program next year

75
00:04:43.041 --> 00:04:46.340
alongside them is promising
the same people twice.

76
00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:48.102
Say that plainly in the plan.

77
00:04:48.102 --> 00:04:51.051
And one clock has already run out:

78
00:04:51.051 --> 00:04:55.528
the legacy telecom contracts
ended by May 31, 2026.

79
00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:58.230
Next, what happens if you don't act.

80
00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:04.155
Not acting doesn't mean standing still.

81
00:05:04.155 --> 00:05:05.880
Three things happen by default.

82
00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:09.043
First, money gets spent
without a choice being made.

83
00:05:09.043 --> 00:05:14.154
The BOD 26-02 refresh will
happen on a directive clock.

84
00:05:14.154 --> 00:05:17.819
Run as like-for-like replacement,
it spends the money and closes

85
00:05:17.819 --> 00:05:21.343
the architecture question for
the life of the new equipment.

86
00:05:21.343 --> 00:05:23.356
Deferral has a measured price too.

87
00:05:23.356 --> 00:05:30.284
GAO reported that billing for six agencies'
legacy Networx contracts rose from about

88
00:05:30.284 --> 00:05:33.992
13.4 million dollars in
February 2025 to about

89
00:05:33.992 --> 00:05:37.505
40.9 million in March,
a 206 percent increase.

90
00:05:37.505 --> 00:05:41.693
Second, gaps surface as findings
rather than accepted risks.

91
00:05:41.693 --> 00:05:46.168
The technical situation is identical;
the institutional position isn't.

92
00:05:46.168 --> 00:05:50.593
And cutting managed TIC circuits
before CISA confirms replacement

93
00:05:50.593 --> 00:05:54.942
telemetry trades a transport gap for
a visibility gap, which is worse.

94
00:05:54.942 --> 00:05:57.481
Third, cost gets blamed on the cloud.

95
00:05:57.481 --> 00:06:00.688
When workloads migrate before
transport is measured,

96
00:06:00.688 --> 00:06:05.180
slowness gets blamed on the only thing
being measured, and cloud spend rises.

97
00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:10.205
The book labels that its own inference,
a hypothesis to test in your estate.

98
00:06:10.205 --> 00:06:13.755
And under all three, the
governor is attack surface.

99
00:06:13.755 --> 00:06:18.674
In February 2026, Emergency
Directive 26-03 ordered action

100
00:06:18.674 --> 00:06:23.493
on Cisco SD-WAN systems after
attackers compromised them.

101
00:06:23.493 --> 00:06:26.729
Whatever you buy, protect it
before it carries traffic.

102
00:06:26.729 --> 00:06:30.260
Next, what TIC 3.0 actually requires.

103
00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:36.460
What does TIC 3.0 actually require?

104
00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:40.473
The book reads it from the current
documents, not from what's said about them.

105
00:06:40.473 --> 00:06:45.532
CISA republished the whole set in
September 2026, but the only changes

106
00:06:45.532 --> 00:06:50.248
were updated footnote links, so
the substance dates from July 2025.

107
00:06:50.248 --> 00:06:55.461
There are four use cases: Traditional,
Branch Office, Remote User and Cloud.

108
00:06:55.461 --> 00:06:57.285
There's no SASE use case.

109
00:06:57.285 --> 00:07:00.571
An agency deploying SASE
at its branches operates

110
00:07:00.571 --> 00:07:03.322
under the Branch Office
and Cloud use cases.

111
00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:06.323
TIC 3.0 sets goals, not methods.

112
00:07:06.323 --> 00:07:08.672
But two statements constrain a branch.

113
00:07:08.672 --> 00:07:13.301
Policy enforcement parity: once
you decide a capability is needed,

114
00:07:13.301 --> 00:07:16.584
you apply it on every path,
SD-WAN paths included.

115
00:07:16.584 --> 00:07:22.260
And preserve CISA's visibility as branches
connect directly to outside services.

116
00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:27.625
CISA's June 2026 SASE guide adds that
traffic doesn't have to route through TIC

117
00:07:27.625 --> 00:07:30.552
access points if an alternative gives both

118
00:07:30.552 --> 00:07:33.560
the agency and CISA situational awareness.

119
00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:35.310
That guide permits SASE.

120
00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:36.735
It doesn't require it.

121
00:07:36.735 --> 00:07:39.758
So when someone says TIC requires SD-WAN,

122
00:07:39.758 --> 00:07:43.698
or that TIC forbids local
breakout, both claims fail.

123
00:07:43.698 --> 00:07:48.605
The honest position is narrower and
stronger: local breakout is allowed,

124
00:07:48.605 --> 00:07:53.922
and the agency must show parity and preserve
CISA's view on every path it opens.

125
00:07:53.922 --> 00:07:56.290
Next, the opposite mistake.

126
00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.483
The opposite mistake is just as common:

127
00:08:02.483 --> 00:08:07.827
concluding that because policy is
technology-neutral, the branch is unconstrained.

128
00:08:07.827 --> 00:08:08.653
It isn't.

129
00:08:08.653 --> 00:08:13.352
Four binding obligations land on branch
equipment, and none of them picks a product.

130
00:08:13.352 --> 00:08:17.185
Egress DNS must go to
CISA's Protective DNS.

131
00:08:17.185 --> 00:08:24.103
Internet-exposed management interfaces
come off the internet under BOD 23-02.

132
00:08:24.103 --> 00:08:29.353
End-of-support edge
devices go under BOD 26-02.

133
00:08:29.353 --> 00:08:37.278
And anything terminating TLS or IPsec needs
post-quantum key exchange under M-26-15.

134
00:08:37.278 --> 00:08:41.878
The obligations that bite hardest are
often ones the agency wrote itself.

135
00:08:41.878 --> 00:08:45.352
Once a branch breaks out locally,
it's an external boundary,

136
00:08:45.352 --> 00:08:48.103
and the security plan
makes promises about it.

137
00:08:48.103 --> 00:08:51.602
That gap surfaces at assessment
time, not deployment time.

138
00:08:51.602 --> 00:08:54.377
For budget owners, the
other side matters too.

139
00:08:54.377 --> 00:08:59.216
No federal instrument requires SD-WAN
or SASE, and none requires the WAN

140
00:08:59.216 --> 00:09:04.053
itself to be encrypted where the
payload is already protected end to end.

141
00:09:04.053 --> 00:09:08.277
So an overlay is a risk decision,
not a compliance obligation.

142
00:09:08.277 --> 00:09:13.415
And FedRAMP certifies cloud services, not
the routes an agency uses to reach them.

143
00:09:13.415 --> 00:09:18.702
When a network change is refused on FedRAMP
grounds, ask which control it violates.

144
00:09:18.702 --> 00:09:22.383
A refusal with no control
basis is a governance failure,

145
00:09:22.383 --> 00:09:24.303
and should be recorded as one.

146
00:09:24.303 --> 00:09:26.810
Next, logging and telemetry.

147
00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:33.460
Logging and CISA
telemetry are where branch

148
00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:36.360
modernization most often
goes wrong quietly.

149
00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:44.310
M-26-14 rescinded M-21-31 on May 22, 2026.

150
00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:48.577
Logs must be searchable for six
months and retrievable for a year,

151
00:09:48.577 --> 00:09:53.160
and agencies must be able to show
addresses, ports and session details.

152
00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:56.585
A branch with nothing at its edge
doesn't produce that record.

153
00:09:56.585 --> 00:09:59.557
When branches leave the
managed TIC service,

154
00:09:59.557 --> 00:10:02.610
the telemetry CISA has
been receiving stops.

155
00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:06.335
The replacement is CLAW, CISA's
log aggregation warehouse,

156
00:10:06.335 --> 00:10:10.973
and onboarding takes the agency, CISA
and the provider working together.

157
00:10:10.973 --> 00:10:14.615
That's a three-party dependency
with its own lead time,

158
00:10:14.615 --> 00:10:18.259
so it belongs on the critical
path, starting in Phase 0.

159
00:10:18.259 --> 00:10:19.460
Here's the decision.

160
00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:24.451
The exit criterion for leaving MTIPS,
and for ending each branch's MPLS,

161
00:10:24.451 --> 00:10:27.635
is confirmed CLAW parity,
not branch cutover.

162
00:10:27.635 --> 00:10:31.760
Cutting circuits first trades a
transport gap for a visibility gap.

163
00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:34.497
Ownership splits three ways on purpose.

164
00:10:34.497 --> 00:10:37.247
The CISO decides when parity is reached.

165
00:10:37.247 --> 00:10:40.872
Security operations confirms
it, branch by branch.

166
00:10:40.872 --> 00:10:44.257
And only a contracting officer
can terminate a contract,

167
00:10:44.257 --> 00:10:48.722
so the contracting officer ends the
circuit, only after that confirmation.

168
00:10:48.722 --> 00:10:52.723
No engineer can cancel a circuit,
however redundant it looks.

169
00:10:52.723 --> 00:10:54.880
Next, the long interim.

170
00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:03.805
For many agencies, neither SD-WAN
nor a new TIC use case is close.

171
00:11:03.805 --> 00:11:07.443
The acquisition isn't funded and
the task orders aren't placed.

172
00:11:07.443 --> 00:11:10.371
The book calls that
interval the long interim,

173
00:11:10.371 --> 00:11:14.918
and argues the architecture by
elimination, because no mandate exists.

174
00:11:14.918 --> 00:11:18.856
The hairpin is still permitted,
but CISA's own guidebook says it

175
00:11:18.856 --> 00:11:23.017
raised the cost and cut the
effectiveness of TIC for cloud services.

176
00:11:23.017 --> 00:11:27.505
A new agency-run access point is
a capital and staffing commitment.

177
00:11:27.505 --> 00:11:30.705
VPN backhaul is a hairpin
under another name.

178
00:11:30.705 --> 00:11:35.013
What's left is local breakout, a
cloud-delivered security stack nearby,

179
00:11:35.013 --> 00:11:37.242
and a policy layer at the branch edge.

180
00:11:37.242 --> 00:11:42.369
The target is years away for structural
reasons: acquisition takes quarters,

181
00:11:42.369 --> 00:11:45.172
budget submissions go to OMB in September,

182
00:11:45.172 --> 00:11:49.818
and a thousand branches is a multi-year
deployment on logistics alone.

183
00:11:49.818 --> 00:11:51.467
But the interval isn't empty.

184
00:11:51.467 --> 00:11:56.018
Four of the six branch-edge functions
are partly obtainable before any award.

185
00:11:56.018 --> 00:11:58.968
The decision belongs to
the authorizing official.

186
00:11:58.968 --> 00:12:03.679
Record which control assertions depend on
transport evidence that doesn't exist,

187
00:12:03.679 --> 00:12:07.005
the compensating measures, and
the funded plan to fix them.

188
00:12:07.005 --> 00:12:10.880
An undocumented gap found by
an assessor is a finding.

189
00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:14.293
A documented gap with a
plan is an accepted risk.

190
00:12:14.293 --> 00:12:15.180
Do it now.

191
00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:18.060
Next, the most important planning item.

192
00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:24.760
This is the most important
planning item in the book.

193
00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:33.385
BOD 26-02, issued February 5, 2026, puts
branch edge hardware on a directive clock.

194
00:12:33.385 --> 00:12:39.382
Devices whose support ends by February
2027 come out then, and all identified

195
00:12:39.382 --> 00:12:44.910
end-of-support edge devices must be
off agency networks by August 5, 2027.

196
00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:47.876
It covers internet-reachable
boundary devices,

197
00:12:47.876 --> 00:12:51.510
so a branch router with its
own internet circuit qualifies.

198
00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:53.699
An interior access switch may not,

199
00:12:53.699 --> 00:12:57.060
so don't assume the directive
funds the access layer.

200
00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:02.008
For an agency with aging branch equipment,
part of the capital it assumed it didn't

201
00:13:02.008 --> 00:13:06.660
have is going to be spent anyway, inside
the window everyone calls years away.

202
00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:11.010
And a refresh is exactly when a branch
architecture gets chosen again.

203
00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:13.891
The default is like-for-like,
which spends the money

204
00:13:13.891 --> 00:13:16.910
and closes the question for
the life of the equipment.

205
00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:19.635
The recommendation is narrow and cheap.

206
00:13:19.635 --> 00:13:23.270
Tell whoever is scoping the
replacements, in writing,

207
00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:28.475
what the eventual target needs from a
branch device, from application-aware

208
00:13:28.475 --> 00:13:34.010
steering and telemetry to post-quantum
key exchange and a FIPS 140-3 expectation.

209
00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:38.903
Security architecture owns that decision,
before the replacements are scoped,

210
00:13:38.903 --> 00:13:41.572
and acquisition writes
it into every order.

211
00:13:41.572 --> 00:13:43.747
It costs requirements-writing time.

212
00:13:43.747 --> 00:13:47.122
Not doing it spends the same
money and closes the option.

213
00:13:47.122 --> 00:13:49.280
Next, where to invest.

214
00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:55.480
Where should the money
go, and in what order?

215
00:13:55.480 --> 00:14:00.818
The book lines its chapters up into five
tiers, cheapest and most certain first.

216
00:14:00.818 --> 00:14:03.100
First, staff time, with no award:

217
00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:09.126
write the application-to-path policy the
controls already ask for, instrument a

218
00:14:09.126 --> 00:14:15.243
sample of branches, and test, not just read,
that DNS reaches CISA's Protective DNS.

219
00:14:15.243 --> 00:14:21.804
Second, requirements time: put the
target into the BOD 26-02 refresh.

220
00:14:21.804 --> 00:14:24.730
Third, accreditation and CLAW onboarding.

221
00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.823
Circuits arrive in about a quarter;

222
00:14:26.823 --> 00:14:30.142
an authorized breakout
architecture takes far longer.

223
00:14:30.142 --> 00:14:34.417
Fourth, protect the governor,
with phishing-resistant MFA,

224
00:14:34.417 --> 00:14:38.068
hardening, fast patching
and logs held off the box.

225
00:14:38.068 --> 00:14:40.292
Fifth, and only then, capital.

226
00:14:40.292 --> 00:14:41.580
One point on pricing.

227
00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:47.033
Moving from MPLS to internet access plus
a cloud security service changes the cost

228
00:14:47.033 --> 00:14:52.012
driver from site count to user count,
and the per-user security subscription

229
00:14:52.012 --> 00:14:56.280
is the line most likely to dominate,
and the one most often left out.

230
00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:01.830
Price your own circuits in GSA's EIS pricer
rather than citing a marketing number.

231
00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:04.876
And don't justify the
program on latency alone;

232
00:15:04.876 --> 00:15:08.242
the stronger argument is
governance and evidence.

233
00:15:08.242 --> 00:15:10.311
The sponsor and CIO set this order,

234
00:15:10.311 --> 00:15:14.668
and it needs to be settled before
the next September budget submission.

235
00:15:14.668 --> 00:15:17.260
Next, a worked field-office case.

236
00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:24.648
Field offices are where these
decisions become a budget line.

237
00:15:24.648 --> 00:15:28.945
The book works a case many
civilian agencies will recognize:

238
00:15:28.945 --> 00:15:34.188
about 1,500 field offices, about 70,000
endpoints, and a request for about 50

239
00:15:34.188 --> 00:15:37.368
million dollars to refresh a Configuration

240
00:15:37.368 --> 00:15:40.548
Manager distribution
point in every office.

241
00:15:40.548 --> 00:15:41.747
Three findings.

242
00:15:41.747 --> 00:15:44.585
First, the unit cost doesn't reconcile.

243
00:15:44.585 --> 00:15:50.519
That's 33,333 dollars per office, about
twice the book's fully loaded server case,

244
00:15:50.519 --> 00:15:55.722
so the request needs to say whether
it's acquisition or five-year cost.

245
00:15:55.722 --> 00:15:57.261
Second, capacity.

246
00:15:57.261 --> 00:16:02.534
Microsoft's published limit is 4,000
clients per distribution point,

247
00:16:02.534 --> 00:16:05.772
so the estate needs 24,
two at each of 12 hubs.

248
00:16:05.772 --> 00:16:11.485
The other 1,476 exist only to
make up for slow branch circuits.

249
00:16:11.485 --> 00:16:12.910
Third, timing.

250
00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:17.400
Microsoft says new device-management
innovation happens in Intune,

251
00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:21.023
so hardware bought now
depreciates past its likely use.

252
00:16:21.023 --> 00:16:23.572
The alternative fixes the circuit instead.

253
00:16:23.572 --> 00:16:27.450
On the book's planning assumptions,
it needs about 10 million dollars

254
00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:31.260
of first-year capital and turns
cumulatively positive in year four.

255
00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:33.331
But it hinges on one number:

256
00:16:33.331 --> 00:16:38.510
breakeven is 873 dollars of current
MPLS cost per office per month.

257
00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:41.885
Below that, argue capability
rather than savings.

258
00:16:41.885 --> 00:16:45.708
So the sponsor and CIO decompose
the request and validate

259
00:16:45.708 --> 00:16:49.372
that MPLS figure before the
refresh money is committed.

260
00:16:49.372 --> 00:16:52.050
Next, network access control.

261
00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:59.025
Network access control looks
like an engineering topic.

262
00:16:59.025 --> 00:17:02.262
It reaches the executive agenda
because it's usually the

263
00:17:02.262 --> 00:17:05.775
last piece of the network still
standing on Active Directory.

264
00:17:05.775 --> 00:17:09.881
Windows Network Policy Server, the
RADIUS server built into Windows,

265
00:17:09.881 --> 00:17:13.770
authorizes devices against Active
Directory computer objects,

266
00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:16.363
and Entra joined devices
don't support that.

267
00:17:16.363 --> 00:17:18.388
There's no Entra RADIUS service.

268
00:17:18.388 --> 00:17:22.987
So as the Entra migration
progresses, NPS becomes less useful.

269
00:17:22.987 --> 00:17:26.431
And if it goes away before
its replacement is validated,

270
00:17:26.431 --> 00:17:28.826
the result isn't a degraded experience.

271
00:17:28.826 --> 00:17:31.175
It's a total loss of network access.

272
00:17:31.175 --> 00:17:34.813
The older password methods are
breaking on their own schedule, too.

273
00:17:34.813 --> 00:17:39.416
Credential Guard and the October 2026
Windows update are closing them off,

274
00:17:39.416 --> 00:17:43.574
so agencies on older guidance now
have a deadline they didn't choose.

275
00:17:43.574 --> 00:17:49.060
The target is certificate-based
authentication for every managed device, with

276
00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:50.806
posture read from Intune,

277
00:17:50.806 --> 00:17:54.712
rolled out over roughly 90 to
180 days, monitor mode first.

278
00:17:54.712 --> 00:17:59.769
One caution: as of October 21, 2026,
several well-known cloud RADIUS

279
00:17:59.769 --> 00:18:05.575
offerings have no FedRAMP listing, so
confirm before designing around one.

280
00:18:05.575 --> 00:18:10.637
The sponsor and CIO choose the
platform and decide when NPS retires.

281
00:18:10.637 --> 00:18:13.503
The trigger is the Entra
migration, not a date,

282
00:18:13.503 --> 00:18:16.213
and the replacement
must be in place first.

283
00:18:16.213 --> 00:18:18.575
The CISO sets the posture requirement.

284
00:18:18.575 --> 00:18:20.870
Next, who owns all of this.

285
00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:27.359
The book's sharpest
observation is that the most

286
00:18:27.359 --> 00:18:30.508
expensive network mistakes
aren't engineering errors.

287
00:18:30.508 --> 00:18:34.931
They're ownership gaps: a circuit bought
before anyone owned the governor,

288
00:18:34.931 --> 00:18:37.976
a cutover before anyone
filed the boundary change,

289
00:18:37.976 --> 00:18:42.471
a telemetry contract cancelled before
anyone confirmed its replacement.

290
00:18:42.471 --> 00:18:46.295
Naming one decider for each step
is cheaper than any product.

291
00:18:46.295 --> 00:18:48.770
The register separates
deciding from doing.

292
00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:53.720
Network engineering does most of the work,
but decides only physics and phasing.

293
00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:59.009
The sponsor and CIO decide the governor
platforms and the order of branch waves,

294
00:18:59.009 --> 00:19:02.295
because those fix cost and
the boundary for years.

295
00:19:02.295 --> 00:19:06.070
The CISO sets the security
envelope every order carries.

296
00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:11.220
Security architecture decides the refresh
attributes and where each control sits.

297
00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:16.020
The authorizing official accepts risk,
so four rows land there, including the

298
00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:21.120
boundary change, decided before the first
production cutover, and the interim gap.

299
00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:22.882
Two rows split on purpose.

300
00:19:22.882 --> 00:19:27.995
The authorizing official decides whether
the boundary may change, but the system

301
00:19:27.995 --> 00:19:32.957
owner decides, site by site, whether that
site's control layer is live and logging.

302
00:19:32.957 --> 00:19:35.411
And telemetry splits between the CISO,

303
00:19:35.411 --> 00:19:38.533
security operations and
the contracting officer.

304
00:19:38.533 --> 00:19:41.464
When a refusal arrives
with no control basis,

305
00:19:41.464 --> 00:19:45.583
record it as a governance failure,
with the refusing role named.

306
00:19:45.583 --> 00:19:47.690
Next, how to proceed.

307
00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:53.603
How to proceed comes down to order.

308
00:19:53.603 --> 00:19:58.040
Order decides whether branch
modernization is a gain or a regression.

309
00:19:58.040 --> 00:19:59.965
First, secure the governor.

310
00:19:59.965 --> 00:20:03.999
The SD-WAN controller is internet-facing
with estate-wide authority,

311
00:20:03.999 --> 00:20:06.618
so it comes under
vulnerability management,

312
00:20:06.618 --> 00:20:10.440
privileged access and off-box
logging before it carries traffic.

313
00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:12.652
Then buy the pipe on measured physics.

314
00:20:12.652 --> 00:20:15.672
Deploy the governor before
production traffic:

315
00:20:15.672 --> 00:20:20.277
a site whose control layer isn't live
and logging doesn't get its new egress.

316
00:20:20.277 --> 00:20:23.790
Treat the boundary change as
the significant change it is,

317
00:20:23.790 --> 00:20:25.839
decided before the first cutover.

318
00:20:25.839 --> 00:20:30.353
And confirm CISA telemetry parity
before any legacy circuit ends.

319
00:20:30.353 --> 00:20:34.527
The book is candid that no memo
or directive sets this sequence.

320
00:20:34.527 --> 00:20:39.378
It's an engineering dependency, and saying
so is what makes it survive review.

321
00:20:39.378 --> 00:20:43.361
Lighting up fast circuits before
the security layer is live is,

322
00:20:43.361 --> 00:20:47.652
in the program's experience, the
most common and most costly mistake.

323
00:20:47.652 --> 00:20:51.428
When funding becomes possible,
prove the case in your own estate.

324
00:20:51.428 --> 00:20:57.003
Instrument two comparable branches for 30
days, change one, and measure 30 more.

325
00:20:57.003 --> 00:21:00.089
That's an effect size a
budget examiner will accept.

326
00:21:00.089 --> 00:21:02.682
The decision: adopt this order as program

327
00:21:02.682 --> 00:21:05.502
policy before the first
production cutover.

328
00:21:05.502 --> 00:21:07.750
Finally, what can start now.

329
00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:15.425
Let me close with what can start now,
because most of it needs no award.

330
00:21:15.425 --> 00:21:18.300
The staff work: write
the application-to-path

331
00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:21.175
routing policy the
controls already ask for.

332
00:21:21.175 --> 00:21:25.100
Its absence is the finding,
not the absence of a device.

333
00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:29.483
Instrument a sample of branches
before the next migration wave,

334
00:21:29.483 --> 00:21:32.212
and verify that DNS really ends at CISA.

335
00:21:32.212 --> 00:21:36.291
Before money moves: send the
target attributes, in writing,

336
00:21:36.291 --> 00:21:39.435
to whoever scopes the
BOD 26-02 replacements,

337
00:21:39.435 --> 00:21:44.874
and ask the field-office request to
separate acquisition from five-year cost.

338
00:21:44.874 --> 00:21:49.175
Before cutover: start CLAW coordination
with CISA and the provider,

339
00:21:49.175 --> 00:21:52.570
have the authorizing official
record the interim gap

340
00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:55.512
as an accepted risk, and
name one decider per row.

341
00:21:55.512 --> 00:21:56.912
One point on urgency.

342
00:21:56.912 --> 00:22:00.767
The branch measurement baseline
can't be reconstructed after

343
00:22:00.767 --> 00:22:04.399
the next migration wave, and it
costs staff time, not capital.

344
00:22:04.399 --> 00:22:08.018
So, the seven decisions: MTIPS
ends on confirmed parity,

345
00:22:08.018 --> 00:22:12.843
the interim gap becomes an accepted
risk, the refresh carries the target,

346
00:22:12.843 --> 00:22:17.667
investment starts with staff time,
the field-office request gets tested,

347
00:22:17.667 --> 00:22:22.813
NPS retirement follows the Entra migration,
and the sequence becomes policy.

348
00:22:22.813 --> 00:22:25.850
Buy transport on physics
and govern it as compliance,

349
00:22:25.850 --> 00:22:28.750
and the branch gets faster
without getting weaker.

350
00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:32.744
Network and Transport has the
detail, every claim footnoted,

351
00:22:32.744 --> 00:22:35.300
and the backup slides list the sources.

352
00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:36.120
Thank you.
